MFEM NURBS mesh v1.0

#
# MFEM Geometry Types (see fem/geom.hpp):
#
# SEGMENT     = 1
# SQUARE      = 3
# CUBE        = 5
#
# Coarse control mesh for mesh-flap.geo
# (perpendicular flap standing on the bottom wall)
#
# y2  +----+----+----+
#     | E2 | E3 | E4 |
# y1  +----+----+----+
#     | E0 |flap| E1 |
# y0  +----+----+----+
#    x0   x1   x2   x3
#
# x0=-3.0  x1=-0.05  x2=0.05  x3=3.0
# y0= 0.0  y1= 1.0   y2=4.0
#
# Linear (order 1) NURBS patches; refine at run time with -r.
#
# Boundary attributes (matching mesh-flap.geo physical curves):
#   1 = inlet, 20 = outlet0, 21 = outlet1,
#   3 = flap,  4  = lowerWall, 5 = upperWall
#
# The knot vectors are pre-graded (not uniform [0,0,1,1]) so that
# element aspect ratios stay comparable across patches under further
# uniform refinement (-r), without chasing an exact 1:1 cell-size
# match (which would require ~30 elements across the wide columns
# just to match E3's narrow width, and keep the coarse mesh needlessly
# large everywhere). Element E3, directly above the flap, is only 0.1
# wide but 3.0 tall; its neighbors are ~2.95 wide and 1.0-3.0 tall.
# Without any grading, uniform refinement keeps every patch's element
# count in lock-step, so E3's cells end up ~30x smaller in area than
# its neighbors' at every refinement level. Instead:
#   kv0 (wide columns, width 2.95):  8 elements
#   kv1 (narrow column, width 0.1):  1 element
#   kv2 (lower row, height 1.0):     1 element
#   kv3 (upper row, height 3.0):     3 elements
# This gives a coarse element aspect ratio of ~3.7:1 (max/min area),
# constant under further -r refinement -- a large improvement over
# the ungraded ~30:1, at a fraction of the element count of an exact
# 1:1 match.
# This was generated with mesh.KnotInsert() from a trivial coarse
# mesh and printed back out, so the control point coordinates and
# DOF numbering are computed and verified by MFEM itself.
#

